    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d862">

                <pb facs="0918" n="906" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg906"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.613/1171: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-08-11T13:00:00+03:00">August 11, 1941—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 12—9:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1470. Victor V. Shiffer, a Soviet member of the Embassy staff now stationed at
                    Kazan left his quarters early on the evening of August 7 for a walk and did not
                    return. Dickerson subsequently ascertained from the local authorities that
                    Shiffer had been detained and sent to Moscow by the “decision” of the Central
                    authorities without the knowledge of the local government.<anchor n="47"
                        xml:id="d862-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>I have addressed a note to the People’s Commissariat for Foreign Affairs
                    reporting this occurrence, requesting that an inquiry be made, and that I be
                    advised the outcome.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d862-f1" n="47" xml:id="d862-f1-content">
                <p>For correspondence concerning earlier arrests of Soviet employees of the American
                    Embassy in Moscow, see <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_319">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939,
                        pp. 319</ref>–<ref target="frus1933-39#pg_320">320</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_375">375</ref>, <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_392"
                        >392</ref>, <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_400">400</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_494">494</ref>, <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_631"
                        >631</ref>, and <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_635">635</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_638">638</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
